# Rust
/target
**/*.rs.bk

# Node
node_modules
dist
dist-ssr
*.local

# Tauri generates these next to the app; they are per machine.
**/src-tauri/gen/schemas

# Android build output. `gen/android` itself IS tracked: it is what
# `tauri android init` produces and it carries the hand-written wiring described
# in docs/BUILD.md §4.3.
**/src-tauri/gen/android/.gradle
**/src-tauri/gen/android/build
**/src-tauri/gen/android/app/build
**/src-tauri/gen/android/local.properties
**/src-tauri/gen/android/.idea
**/src-tauri/gen/android/app/src/main/jniLibs
# Any Android library module under apps/*/plugins/ (currently just `bridge`).
**/plugins/*/build
**/plugins/*/.gradle

# Kotlin 2.x drops compiler session files (and daemon logs) into `.kotlin/` next
# to the build. They are regenerated on every compile and carry a random number
# in the name, so without this every build dirties `git status`.
**/.kotlin

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets - a safety net, not a fix.
#
# Nothing here should ever exist in the working tree: the release signing config
# reads its four values from the *global* `~/.gradle/gradle.properties` or the
# environment (docs/BUILD.md §4.1), and the device identity is generated into the
# per-user app config directory at first run. These patterns exist so that the
# day someone drops a keystore into the repo by hand, git refuses it instead of
# committing it.
#
# A committed signing key cannot be un-leaked by deleting it later: it would
# still be in the history, and in every clone. If this ever fires, rotate the key
# rather than rewriting history.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.jks
*.keystore
keystore.properties
.env
.env.*

# Logs
logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# Editor directories and files
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea
*.suo
*.ntvs*
*.njsproj
*.sln
*.sw?

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
